      },
      "hints": [
        "For a parametric curve, use $\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{dy/d\\theta}{dx/d\\theta}$.",
        "Differentiate $x=2\\sin\\theta+\\sin2\\theta$, including the chain-rule factor in the double-angle term.",
        "Differentiate $y=2\\cos\\theta+\\cos2\\theta$ in the same way.",
        "Divide the two derivatives and simplify any common numerical factor."
      ],
      "walkthrough": [
        "Differentiate the parametric equations with respect to $\\theta$:\n$$\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}\\theta} = \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}\\theta}(2\\sin\\theta + \\sin 2\\theta) = 2\\cos\\theta + 2\\cos 2\\theta$$\n$$\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}\\theta} = \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}\\theta}(2\\cos\\theta + \\cos 2\\theta) = -2\\sin\\theta - 2\\sin 2\\theta$$",
        "Apply the chain rule $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y/\\mathrm{d}\\theta}{\\mathrm{d}x/\\mathrm{d}\\theta}$:\n$$\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{-2\\sin\\theta - 2\\sin 2\\theta}{2\\cos\\theta + 2\\cos 2\\theta} = -\\dfrac{2(\\sin\\theta + \\sin 2\\theta)}{2(\\cos\\theta + \\cos 2\\theta)} = -\\dfrac{\\sin\\theta + \\sin 2\\theta}{\\cos\\theta + \\cos 2\\theta}$$"
      ],
      "checking": {
        "mode": "ai",
        "ai_rubric": {
          "Differentiating Parametric Curves": "Derives $\\frac{dx}{d\\theta}=2\\cos\\theta+2\\cos2\\theta$ or $\\frac{dy}{d\\theta}=-2\\sin\\theta-2\\sin2\\theta$. Applies $dy/dx=(dy/d\\theta)\\div(dx/d\\theta)$. The final result is $\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac{2\\sin\\theta+2\\sin2\\theta}{2\\cos\\theta+2\\cos2\\theta}$."
        },
        "criteria_source": "mark_scheme_structured"
