      "hints": [
        "A tangent parallel to the $y$-axis occurs when $\\frac{dx}{d\\theta}=0$ while $\\frac{dy}{d\\theta}\\ne0$.",
        "Set the denominator from part (i) equal to zero and replace $\\cos2\\theta$ by $2\\cos^2\\theta-1$.",
        "Solve the resulting quadratic in $\\cos\\theta$, retaining the value compatible with $0<\\theta<\\pi$.",
        "Substitute the corresponding exact sine and cosine values into both parametric equations."
      ],
      "walkthrough": [
        "The tangent is parallel to the $y$-axis when the gradient is vertical, which occurs when $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}\\theta} = 0$ (and $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}\\theta} \\ne 0$):\n$$2\\cos\\theta + 2\\cos 2\\theta = 0$$\n$$\\cos\\theta + \\cos 2\\theta = 0$$",
        "Apply the double angle identity $\\cos 2\\theta = 2\\cos^2\\theta - 1$:\n$$2\\cos^2\\theta + \\cos\\theta - 1 = 0$$",
        "Factorize the quadratic in $\\cos\\theta$:\n$$(2\\cos\\theta - 1)(\\cos\\theta + 1) = 0$$\n$$\\cos\\theta = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\quad \\text{or} \\quad \\cos\\theta = -1$$",
        "Select the valid solution in the open interval $0 < \\theta < \\pi$:\n- $\\cos\\theta = -1 \\implies \\theta = \\pi$ (excluded as $\\theta < \\pi$).\n- $\\cos\\theta = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\implies \\theta = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}$.",
        "Check that $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}\\theta} \\ne 0$ at $\\theta = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}$:\n$$-2\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right) - 2\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}\\right) = -2\\left(\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) - 2\\left(\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) = -2\\sqrt{3} \\ne 0$$",
        "Calculate the coordinates $(x, y)$ at $\\theta = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}$:\n$$x = 2\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right) + \\sin\\left(\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}\\right) = 2\\left(\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) + \\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} = \\sqrt{3} + \\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} = \\dfrac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}$$\n$$y = 2\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right) + \\cos\\left(\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}\\right) = 2\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right) + \\left(-\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right) = 1 - \\dfrac{1}{2} = \\dfrac{1}{2}$$",
        "State the exact coordinates:\n$$\\left(\\dfrac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}, \\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)$$"
